            <ArticleTitle>Thinness, overweight and obesity in indigenous youth in Oaxaca, 1970 and 2007.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>387-93</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0036-36342013000500004</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate change in body mass index (BMI) and weight status of indigenous youth in Oaxaca between the 1970s and 2007.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Heights and weights were measured in cross-sectional samples of school children 6-14 years in the 1970s (2 897) and 2007 (4 305); BMI was calculated. International Obesity Task Force cutoffs for weight status were used. BMI and prevalence of severe and moderate thinness, overweight and obesity were compared by year.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">BMI increased significantly across time. Primary change in weight status occurred in overweight, 1970s, &lt;2%; 2007, 7 to 12%. Little change occurred in thinness (&lt;2%) and obesity (≤ 1%) in both surveys, except in children 6-9 years (obesity=4% in 2007).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">BMI and prevalence of overweight increased across all ages from the 1970s to 2007, but children 6-9 years appeared to be more at risk for obesity than youth 10-14 years. Prevalence of thinness was unchanged.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
